
✅ Topics and Partitions

[ Topics ]
🔹Act as logical categories for messages, organizing them by topic name.

[ Partitions ]
🔹Each topic consists of one or more partitions, horizontally scaling data
and enabling parallel processing.

🔹Messages are distributed across partitions, improving read/write throughput
 and fault tolerance.


✅ Log Segments

🔹Partitions are further divided into log segments for efficient writes and deletes.

🔹Each segment is immutable, ensuring data integrity and simplifying recovery.

🔹Messages are only appended to segments, never modified or overwritten.

👉 The obvious question is, how is cleanup done?

🔹Older segments are automatically removed based on retention policies and
 compaction strategies.

🔸Log Cleaner
Removes old log segments based on configured retention and compaction settings.

🔸Compaction
Compacts old segments by removing duplicates and re-writing data in a more
efficient format.


✅ Replication

🔹Data is replicated across multiple brokers for fault tolerance.

🔹Replication Factor - Configures the number of replicas for each partition.

🔹One replica designated as "leader" serves reads/writes, while others
("followers") synchronize data asynchronously.

🔹If a leader fails, a follower becomes the new leader, minimizing downtime.

✅ Offsets and Ordering

🔹Consumers track their progress within a partition using offsets (positions).

🔹Clients periodically commit offsets, indicating what messages they've consumed.

🔹Message ordering is preserved within each partition, not globally across partitions.


👉 [ Storage Tiers ]

🔸Primary Storage
Messages reside on high-performance disks for fast access and frequent reads/writes.

🔸Secondary Storage
Messages can be moved to cheaper, slower storage (e.g., S3) for long-term archiving.



