14:how do handle the back pressure in kafka?
Handling backpressure in Apache Kafka is about managing situations where
 the producer is sending messages faster than the consumer (or broker)
can handle, or consumers are lagging behind.Kafka doesn’t provide automatic
 backpressure handling like some reactive systems,
but you can implement strategies on both the producer and consumer sides.

✅ What is Backpressure in Kafka?
Backpressure happens when:
Producers overwhelm brokers or topics.
Consumers can’t keep up with the incoming data.

🔧 Strategies to Handle Backpressure
📌 1. Producer-side Backpressure
✅ a. Tune acks, retries, linger.ms, batch.size

acks=all
retries=3
linger.ms=50
batch.size=32768
acks=all makes producers wait for confirmation from all in-sync replicas.
linger.ms and batch.size let you buffer messages, reducing pressure on brokers.

📌 2. Consumer-side Backpressure
✅ a. Control max.poll.records
Limit the number of records returned in a poll:

max.poll.records=100
This prevents the consumer from getting too many messages at once,
giving it time to process the batch before fetching more.

✅ b. Increase processing parallelism
Use multi-threading or thread pools to process records concurrently.

Make sure processing time is faster than consumption rate.

✅ c. Tune fetch.min.bytes, fetch.max.bytes
Control how much data is fetched per poll from the broker.

